{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"rescue-dog-adoption-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Adopt a rescue dog","description":"Takes a household from deciding to adopt a rescue dog to a settled dog in gated steps - readiness, choosing a rescue and a dog, home preparation, the first days and the first three months.","category":"pet-care","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["discover","plan","operate"],"role":["individual","parent"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","preferences"],"output":["plan","checklist","questions"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","depth":"specific","tags":["rescue-dog","dog-adoption","decompression","homecoming","adoption-application","settling-in"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["choose-pet-for-lifestyle","plan-new-pet-care","crate-train-dog","introduce-pets","train-dog-behavior"],"personas":["pet-care-advisor"]},"args":[{"name":"household","description":"Who lives with you (ages of children, other pets), your home and garden, whether you rent and what the landlord allows, work pattern and hours the dog would be alone, and your country.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"hopes","description":"What you hope for in a dog (size, energy, age), your experience with dogs, budget, and any dog you have already seen. Optional.","type":"text"}],"steps":[{"id":"readiness","file":"steps/01-readiness.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"adoption/01-readiness.md"},{"id":"choosing","file":"steps/02-choosing.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"adoption/02-choosing.md"},{"id":"home-prep","file":"steps/03-home-prep.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"adoption/03-home-prep.md"},{"id":"first-days","file":"steps/04-first-days.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","artifact":"adoption/04-first-days.md"},{"id":"first-months","file":"steps/05-first-months.md","stage":"operate","gate":"none","artifact":"adoption/05-first-months.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Guides an adoption the way an experienced rescue coordinator would: be honest about readiness, choose the dog for the household you actually have rather than the photo, prepare before the dog arrives, keep the first days quiet, and build trust and training over months. Each step writes one artifact and stops for approval.\n\n<household>\n{{household}}\n</household>\n{{#hopes}}<hopes>\n{{hopes}}\n</hopes>{{/hopes}}\n\nRules for every step:\n- Use only what the household told you. Ask for missing essentials and mark gaps as [X].\n- Rescue processes, adoption fees and rules (licensing, breed restrictions, tenancy terms) differ by country and organisation; mark them [CHECK] and say who to ask.\n- Reward-based methods only; never suggest punishment or aversive tools.\n- Health concerns go to a vet; do not diagnose or suggest medicines. Serious behaviour concerns go to a qualified behaviourist.\n- Do not name specific rescues or invent prices.\n- End each artifact with open questions.","steps":[{"id":"readiness","file":"steps/01-readiness.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"adoption/01-readiness.md","text":"# Step 1: Readiness\n\n1. Check the hard limits: landlord or building permission, time the dog would be alone each day, allergies, other pets, children's ages, and the next 10 to 15 years of plans.\n2. List the yearly and one-off costs to budget for (food, vet care, insurance, walker or daycare, equipment, training), without inventing amounts.\n3. Say honestly whether now is a good time, and what would make it workable (a walker, a different age of dog, waiting).\n\nSections: Hard limits, Costs to budget, Is now the right time, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"choosing","file":"steps/02-choosing.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"adoption/02-choosing.md","text":"# Step 2: Choosing a rescue and a dog\n\n1. Describe the dog that fits this household: energy, size, age, and how they must be with children, cats or other dogs. Explain why an adult with a known temperament often suits first-time owners.\n2. How to find and judge a rescue: home checks, honest dog profiles, foster-based assessments, after-adoption support, a return policy. Warning signs of a poor rescue or a reseller.\n3. Questions to ask about a specific dog: history, how they are in a home, with children and other animals, alone, on lead, at the vet, any bites, health and medicines.\n4. What to expect from the application and home check, and how to prepare honestly.\n\nSections: The right dog for you, Choosing a rescue, Questions about a dog, Application and home check, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"home-prep","file":"steps/03-home-prep.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"adoption/03-home-prep.md","text":"# Step 3: Preparing the home\n\n1. Supplies list: bed, crate or pen if suitable, harness and lead, ID tag, bowls, the food the dog is used to, chews and toys, baby gates, enzyme cleaner.\n2. Dog-proofing: secure garden and gates, hazards indoors, a quiet base room.\n3. A family agreement: who feeds, walks and trains, house rules, cue words everyone uses, and how children behave around the new dog.\n4. Book a vet registration and first check, and a reward-based trainer if wanted.\n5. Plan the pickup day: travel, a direct route home, a quiet arrival.\n\nSections: Supplies, Dog-proofing, Family agreement, Bookings, Pickup day, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"first-days","file":"steps/04-first-days.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","artifact":"adoption/04-first-days.md","text":"# Step 4: The first days\n\n1. Decompression: why the first days should be calm and quiet; no visitors, no dog parks, short familiar walks, and letting the dog come to people.\n2. A simple daily routine with toilet trips, meals, rest and short walks.\n3. Introductions to resident pets and children, slowly and supervised.\n4. What is normal (sleeping a lot, not eating much, toilet accidents, clinginess or aloofness) and what needs a call to the rescue or vet.\n5. Escape prevention: harness and lead on every trip out, doors and gates checked, ID on.\n\nSections: Decompression, Daily routine, Introductions, Normal and not normal, Escape prevention, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"first-months","file":"steps/05-first-months.md","stage":"operate","gate":"none","artifact":"adoption/05-first-months.md","text":"# Step 5: The first three months\n\n1. What usually changes over the first weeks and months as the dog relaxes, including new behaviours that appear once settled.\n2. A training plan: name, recall in safe spaces, loose lead, settling alone in short steps, and a trainer if needed.\n3. Health: the first vet check, ongoing care to discuss with the vet, and recording changes.\n4. A monthly check-in: what is going well, what is hard, and when to ask the rescue or a behaviourist for help.\n\nSections: What changes, Training plan, Health, Monthly check-in."}]}
